    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A half step immediately above the tonic — "
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " → "
        },
        {
          "text": "b2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — belongs to Phrygian and Locrian only. It leans on home from above, and it's the strongest single darkening move available in the whole set. (What that leaning actually sounds like in Phrygian is a chapter 4 question; here it's just a position on the map.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A half step immediately below the tonic — the major "
        },
        {
          "text": "7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", a leading tone — belongs to Lydian and Ionian only, and it pulls up into home. Every other mode has a "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " there instead, a whole step below — that's five modes, Mixolydian, Dorian, Aeolian, Phrygian and Locrian, and none of them get tugged toward home the way a major key does."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A half step immediately above the fifth — the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — belongs to Aeolian and Phrygian only, and this is the one worth slowing down for. Locrian has a "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " too, but Locrian's fifth is already a "
        },
        {
          "text": "b5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", so the gap between them is a whole step, not a half — Locrian doesn't belong on this list. Dorian's whole point is that it has no half step above the fifth at all: its "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits a whole tone above the "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", where natural minor puts a semitone."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Counted rather than eyeballed: two modes have a half step directly below home (Lydian, Ionian), two have one directly above home (Phrygian, Locrian), three have neither (Mixolydian, Dorian, Aeolian) — and none has both."
        }
      ]
    },
